::Q1::What is the central table in a typical star schema?
{ 
=The fact table
~The category table
~The staging log
~The report table
}

::Q2::What does denormalized mean in the context of a star-schema dimension? 
{
~The fact table contains no foreign keys
=Descriptive attributes are kept together in a flat dimension table rather than split into multiple related tables
~All dimensions are removed from the schema
~Every attribute is stored in a separate database
}

::Q3::Why might category information be placed in a separate table in a snowflake schema? 
{
=The dimension is normalized so related descriptive data is split into separate tables
~Star schemas prohibit product categories
~Snowflake schemas cannot contain dimensions
~The fact table cannot contain foreign keys
}

::Q4::Which schema generally introduces more joins between dimensions and their descriptive subcomponents? 
{
~Fact-only schema
=Snowflake schema
~Star schema
~Neither, because both prohibit relationships
}

::Q5::When might a simpler star schema be attractive? 
{
~When every dimension must be split into many normalized tables
=When straightforward analytical queries and fewer joins are priorities~When no analytical queries will be run
~When a warehouse cannot contain fact tables
}
